.he "diff (1)                     8/16/26                     diff (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
diff
- differential file and directory comparator
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
diff [ options ] file1 file2
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Diff tells what lines must be changed to bring two files into
agreement, or, if both arguments are directories, compares the two
sets of files by name.  Either file may be -, in which case the
standard input is used.
.sp
The default output looks like ed commands: a range with a letter a, d
or c for add, delete or change, followed by the affected lines, those
from file1 flagged < and those from file2 flagged >.
.sp
When both arguments are directories, text files that differ are run
through the file diff, and files found in only one directory, binary
files that differ, and common subdirectories are listed.  If one
argument is a directory and the other a file, the file in the
directory with the same name is used.
.sp
The exit code is 0 for no differences, 1 for some, and 2 for trouble.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-e	Produce an ed script that recreates file2 from file1.

.ti -10
-f	Like -e, but in the opposite order; not useful with ed.

.ti -10
-n	Like -e, but in the opposite order and with a count of changed lines on each command, the form rcsdiff uses.

.ti -10
-c	Produce a diff with lines of context.  The default is 3 lines; -c10 asks for 10.

.ti -10
-h	Do a fast, half-hearted job.  It works only when changed stretches are short and well separated, but does work on files of unlimited length.

.ti -10
-D string	Produce a merged version of the two files with C preprocessor #ifdef string controls, so compiling without string defined yields file1 and with it defined yields file2.

.ti -10
-b	Ignore trailing blanks, and compare other runs of blanks as equal.

.ti -10
-w	Ignore all whitespace.

.ti -10
-i	Ignore the case of letters.

.ti -10
-t	Expand tabs in output lines, to keep the original indentation readable.

.ti -10
-l	For directories, the long format: pipe each text diff through pr and summarize the other differences at the end.

.ti -10
-r	For directories, descend into common subdirectories and compare them too.

.ti -10
-s	Report files that are the same, which are otherwise not mentioned.

.ti -10
-Sname	Start a directory diff at the file name.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The port dropped the -E, -1, -2 and -I companions of -D, which the
v7 diff had; only -D string remains.
.sp
The halfhearted diff is /bin/diffh, not /usr/libexec/diffh, and diff
itself lives at /bin/diff.
.sp
Scripts made with -e or -f are naive about lines consisting of a
single dot.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
cmp (1), ed (1)
